.TH ECHO 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
echo \- write arguments to standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B echo
.RB [ \-neE ]
.RI [ arg ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B echo
writes its arguments to standard output, separated by single spaces and
followed by a newline.
With no arguments it writes only the newline.
It reads no input.
.PP
Arguments are written byte for byte.
In particular, a backslash is an ordinary character unless
.B \-e
is given.
.PP
The kit's shell has
.B echo
as a builtin that runs this same code, so scripts see the behavior
described here; see
.BR sh (1).
Outside the shell, the
.I echo.exe
launcher runs the
.B busybox
applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SS Escape sequences
With
.BR \-e ,
a backslash in an argument starts an escape sequence, which is replaced
as follows.
.TP
.B \ea
Alert (BEL, 0x07).
.TP
.B \eb
Backspace (0x08).
.TP
.B \ec
Stop: write nothing more, not even the newline.
Output from before it is kept, but the rest of this argument and all
later arguments are dropped.
.TP
.B \ee
Escape (0x1B).
.TP
.B \ef
Form feed (0x0C).
.TP
.B \en
Line feed (0x0A).
.TP
.B \er
Carriage return (0x0D).
.TP
.B \et
Tab (0x09).
.TP
.B \ev
Vertical tab (0x0B).
.TP
.B \e\e
Backslash.
.TP
\fB\e0\fInnn\fR, \fB\e\fInnn\fR
The octet whose octal value is
.IR nnn .
Either a
.B \e0
and up to three octal digits, or one to three octal digits the first of
which is 1 to 7.
A value above 255 ends the sequence before the digit that would exceed
it, so
.B \e400
is a space (octal 40) followed by
.BR 0 .
A
.B \e0
that is not followed by an octal digit is a NUL octet.
.TP
.BI \ex HH
The octet whose hexadecimal value is
.IR HH ,
one or two digits in either case.
A
.B \ex
not followed by a hexadecimal digit is written as is.
.PP
A backslash followed by any other character, or ending an argument, is
written as is, and the character after it is then read normally.
So
.B \eE
and
.B \eu0041
are written unchanged.
.SH OPTIONS
Options are recognized only at the start of the arguments, and may be
grouped, as in
.BR \-ne .
An argument is an option only if it is a dash followed by one or more of
the letters
.BR n ,
.BR e ,
and
.BR E .
The first argument that is not, such as
.BR \-\- ,
a lone
.BR \- ,
.BR \-x ,
or
.BR \-nx ,
ends the options: it and all later arguments are written as ordinary
arguments.
Neither
.B \-\-
nor
.B \-\-help
is special: they are written like any other argument.
.TP
.B \-n
Do not write the trailing newline.
.TP
.B \-e
Interpret backslash escape sequences, as described under
.BR "Escape sequences" .
.TP
.B \-E
Do not interpret backslash escape sequences.
This is the default.
It does not cancel an earlier
.BR \-e :
both
.B "echo \-e \-E"
and
.B "echo \-E \-e"
interpret escapes.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
arguments as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ", " BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION
When standard output is a console, decide whether the console or busybox
interprets the escape sequences in the output, such as for color.
See
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B CONEMUPID
When it is set and neither of the above is, busybox uses terminal mode
0: it turns virtual terminal output off and emulates escape sequences
itself.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Standard output could not be written.
A message such as
.B "echo: write error: Bad file descriptor"
is written to standard error.
In the shell's builtin the message begins with the shell's name, such as
.BR sh: ,
instead.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before the output was written.
In the shell's builtin, the shell process running it exits with this status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write a line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo hello world
hello world
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a prompt, leaving the cursor on the same line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \-n \(aqContinue? \(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Without
.BR \-e ,
backslashes are written as is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \(aqone\entwo\(aq
one\entwo
$ echo \-e \(aqone\entwo\(aq
one
two
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a file with CR LF line endings:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \-e \(aqfirst\er\(aq >dos.txt
$ echo \-e \(aqsecond\er\(aq >>dos.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a word in red on a console:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \-e \(aq\ee[31mred\ee[0m\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a string that
.B echo
would take as an option, using
.BR printf (1)
instead:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq%s\en\(aq \-n
\-n
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.B echo
and bash:
.IP \(bu 2
A later
.B \-E
does not turn off an earlier
.BR \-e .
.IP \(bu 2
An octal value above 255 ends the escape sequence early instead of
wrapping modulo 256.
.IP \(bu 2
As in GNU
.B echo
but not bash, an octal escape may omit the leading zero:
.B \e101
is
.BR A .
.IP \(bu 2
The bash escapes
.BR \eE ,
.BR \eu ,
and
.B \eU
are not recognized.
.IP \(bu 2
Unlike GNU
.BR echo ,
there are no
.B \-\-help
or
.B \-\-version
options.
.PP
Standard output is binary, so the newline is a bare LF.
To end a line with CR LF, use
.B \-e
and
.BR \er .
.PP
In
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B echo
is a built-in command that runs instead of the applet, and in PowerShell
it is an alias for
.BR Write-Output .
Run
.B echo.exe
or
.B busybox echo
there.
Since the C runtime then expands wildcards, even in quoted arguments,
.B echo.exe *.c
lists the matching files; set
.B BB_GLOBBING
to
.B 0
to prevent that.
.PP
With
.BR \-e ,
a backslash in a Windows path is an escape: in
.BR C:\etemp ,
.B \et
is a tab.
Write such paths with forward slashes, or leave out
.BR \-e .
In the kit's shell, quote such an argument in single quotes: unquoted,
the shell removes the backslash before
.B echo
sees it.
.PP
Arguments may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903
and later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
When standard output is a console, the output is converted for display
from the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later.
Escape sequences, such as those for color, take effect, and busybox
emulates them on consoles without virtual terminal support.
The whole output is one block, so on a console a NUL octet in it, such
as from
.BR \e0 ,
makes busybox write all of it unconverted and without escape sequence
emulation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR printf (1),
.BR sh (1)
